00:01:11.472 --> 00:01:12.430
RAJEN SHETH: All right.

00:01:12.430 --> 00:01:14.120
Well, thank you all
for coming today.

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:16.120
My name is Rajen
Sheth, and I'm director

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.220
of product management
for Chrome and Android

00:01:18.220 --> 00:01:20.230
in business and education.

00:01:20.230 --> 00:01:22.270
And we want to talk
to you a little bit

00:01:22.270 --> 00:01:26.260
about what we're doing here
at Google in education.

00:01:26.260 --> 00:01:30.880
And so the video
that we just showed

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:32.650
was something that we
produced last year.

00:01:32.650 --> 00:01:35.710
And really, what we've
been keying in on is

00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:37.210
it really does take a teacher.

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:40.240
It takes a teacher to
really make a difference.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:43.785
And so one of the things
that I love about conferences

00:01:43.785 --> 00:01:45.160
like this is I
get to meet people

00:01:45.160 --> 00:01:47.380
from various different
walks of life,

00:01:47.380 --> 00:01:50.080
various different geographical
areas, different age groups,

00:01:50.080 --> 00:01:51.910
all kinds of different things.

00:01:51.910 --> 00:01:54.370
All of us-- one thing
that we all have in common

00:01:54.370 --> 00:01:57.340
is that we were kids
at one point in time.

00:01:57.340 --> 00:01:58.720
We all went to school.

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:02.210
We all had great teachers
that influenced our lives.

00:02:02.210 --> 00:02:05.320
And so what I asked you to do
is to think back to that time.

00:02:05.320 --> 00:02:07.300
Think about a great
teacher-- somebody

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:11.320
that actually made a difference
for you, that has actually

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:14.650
impacted what you've ended
up doing in your career.

00:02:14.650 --> 00:02:17.504
So for me, my
favorite example is

00:02:17.504 --> 00:02:19.420
a guy named Robert
Thomason, who you see here.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:20.890
And there's a
picture of me that's

00:02:20.890 --> 00:02:24.040
a little bit younger
than what I am right now.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:28.480
And Mr. Thomason was my
fifth and sixth grade teacher

00:02:28.480 --> 00:02:30.280
back in Colorado.

00:02:30.280 --> 00:02:33.430
And he came into my life at
a very important juncture.

00:02:33.430 --> 00:02:35.620
When I was in
about fourth grade,

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:38.500
I had a lot of potential, but I
was not showing that potential

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.280
whatsoever.

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:41.770
You know, I was
failing tests, I was

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:45.250
skipping homework assignments,
I was getting bad grades.

00:02:45.250 --> 00:02:47.590
And everybody was worried.

00:02:47.590 --> 00:02:49.810
My parents were worried,
my teachers were worried,

00:02:49.810 --> 00:02:50.500
I was worried.

00:02:50.500 --> 00:02:52.450
I was wondering, could
I cut it at school?

00:02:52.450 --> 00:02:56.890
Would I have the interest in
school to be able to do well?

00:02:56.890 --> 00:02:59.770
And then Mr. Thomason
came in, and he

00:02:59.770 --> 00:03:02.800
changed my view of what
education and what teaching

00:03:02.800 --> 00:03:03.830
is all about.

00:03:03.830 --> 00:03:05.680
So I'll give you some examples.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:09.430
Rather than just doing
multiplication tables,

00:03:09.430 --> 00:03:11.890
Mr. Thomason came in
with a box of staplers.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.110
And he handed us
these staplers and he

00:03:14.110 --> 00:03:17.830
said, hand these out to friends
of yours or to your family,

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:20.080
and ask them what
they think about it.

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:22.570
Get data about do
they really like it?

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:24.230
Do they like it a little bit?

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:26.380
which one do they prefer?

00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:28.040
And then come back
with that data.

00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:30.790
And so we used that to
analyze data and try

00:03:30.790 --> 00:03:33.490
to figure out who
liked what, and be

00:03:33.490 --> 00:03:35.980
able to learn math in that way.

00:03:35.980 --> 00:03:38.260
Rather than just teaching
us about wildlife

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:42.610
and about outdoor
plants, we actually

00:03:42.610 --> 00:03:45.320
took a week-long camping trip
in the Colorado wilderness.

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:47.740
And so we went as
a group and learned

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.740
about how to navigate in
an outdoor environment,

00:03:50.740 --> 00:03:53.770
and learned about animals,
learned about plants firsthand.

00:03:53.770 --> 00:03:55.720
And then also got to
learn about each other

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:57.910
and really have a
bonding experience.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:01.630
Rather than teaching us how to
write a five-paragraph essay,

00:04:01.630 --> 00:04:06.280
Mr. Thomason had us
write persuasive letters

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:08.230
to figures that we
wanted to influence

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:10.850
and to companies whose
products we didn't like.

00:04:10.850 --> 00:04:14.860
And so in my case, I actually
wrote letters to a presidential

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:17.320
candidate-- at that
time, Gary Hart--

00:04:17.320 --> 00:04:20.019
and also wrote to our
state senator, Tim Wirth.

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:22.780
And then I also wrote
to Wesson Oil Company

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:26.140
because my mom's oil bottle
was leaking in the cabinet,

00:04:26.140 --> 00:04:28.320
so I wrote them a
strongly worded letter

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.774
to complain about that.

00:04:30.774 --> 00:04:32.440
Amazingly, I got
replies from everybody.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.020
I got a handwritten letter from
Gary Hart, handwritten letter

00:04:35.020 --> 00:04:38.470
from Tim Wirth, and I
got a stack of coupons

00:04:38.470 --> 00:04:41.020
from Wesson Oil who
I'm sure was pretty

00:04:41.020 --> 00:04:44.950
amused that a 10-year-old was
writing them about a leaky can.

00:04:44.950 --> 00:04:49.150
But these are things that have
stuck with me to this day.

00:04:49.150 --> 00:04:52.570
And it's that kind of thing that
has really inspired me to learn

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:56.080
and inspired me to take
learning into my own hands.

00:04:56.080 --> 00:04:58.300
And these are the
kinds of things that we

00:04:58.300 --> 00:05:00.100
need to reimagine education.

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.860
And so the title of this
talk is a little deceiving.

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:05.410
It's not about technology
reimagining education,

00:05:05.410 --> 00:05:08.500
it's about educators
reimagining education.

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:10.570
And how can we empower
those educators

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:13.120
to do great things
with their kids

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:16.030
to be able to then really
reimagine education?

00:05:16.030 --> 00:05:18.250
And this is even
more important now

00:05:18.250 --> 00:05:20.050
than it ever has
been in the past,

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.360
because a lot of the
things that were important

00:05:22.360 --> 00:05:25.450
when I was growing up in terms
of just getting knowledge

00:05:25.450 --> 00:05:28.370
into your head aren't
nearly as important.

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:31.060
So as an example,
I had to memorize

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:34.150
the list of all the presidents
in the United States.

00:05:34.150 --> 00:05:37.240
And I can recite for
you 1 through 45 who

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:38.600
the presidents are.

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:40.930
However, what I
realized later is

00:05:40.930 --> 00:05:43.420
that it wasn't important to
know that Thomas Jefferson was

00:05:43.420 --> 00:05:45.150
the third president
of the United States.

00:05:45.150 --> 00:05:48.160
It's more important to know
what exactly did he do?

00:05:48.160 --> 00:05:51.640
How did that impact the United
States and the rest of history?

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:52.960
What can we learn from that?

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:55.070
Those are the kinds of
things that are important.

00:05:55.070 --> 00:05:57.575
A lot of the rest
of it we can now--

00:05:57.575 --> 00:06:01.390
any of our kids can now pull
out phones and look them up

00:06:01.390 --> 00:06:03.400
in three seconds,
whereas for us, we'd

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:05.350
have to go to a library
to be able to look up

00:06:05.350 --> 00:06:06.880
that kind of information.

00:06:06.880 --> 00:06:09.240
These are the things that
we need to start to teach.

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:11.540
How do you use that
knowledge as opposed

00:06:11.540 --> 00:06:13.320
to just the knowledge itself?

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:15.450
And in fact, one of the
interesting things we did

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:17.366
is we actually worked
with the Economist Group

00:06:17.366 --> 00:06:21.770
to talk to employers and try to
figure out what types of skills

00:06:21.770 --> 00:06:24.722
do they see as really
necessary to be able to succeed

00:06:24.722 --> 00:06:25.430
in the workplace?

00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:26.930
And these are the
results that came back.

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:28.475
And this is from
senior executives

00:06:28.475 --> 00:06:30.890
of a lot of different companies.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:33.740
The really interesting
thing, if you look at the top

00:06:33.740 --> 00:06:35.990
five, and really
almost all of it--

00:06:35.990 --> 00:06:38.600
very little of it has to
do with the knowledge that

00:06:38.600 --> 00:06:40.010
is in your head.

00:06:40.010 --> 00:06:42.470
All of it has to do with
how you work with each other

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:44.510
and how you use the
knowledge in your head,

00:06:44.510 --> 00:06:46.730
and how do you actually
create new knowledge?

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:49.149
Things like creativity
and critical thinking.

00:06:49.149 --> 00:06:50.690
Those are the kinds
of things that we

00:06:50.690 --> 00:06:52.760
need to be teaching
in schools, and those

00:06:52.760 --> 00:06:55.220
are the kinds of things that
we need to really orient

00:06:55.220 --> 00:06:58.100
our technology towards.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:06:59.660
So one big question
that comes up

00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:04.730
is does technology really
improve educational outcomes?

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:06.950
And that really depends.

00:07:06.950 --> 00:07:09.110
You've seen studies which
show that technology

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:09.989
makes no difference.

00:07:09.989 --> 00:07:12.530
You've seen studies that show
it makes a negative difference.

00:07:12.530 --> 00:07:15.860
Many, many that show that it
makes a positive difference.

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:19.910
And what I really think is
it's asking the wrong question,

00:07:19.910 --> 00:07:23.210
because technology is
not the driver here.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:25.640
It's about how you
use that technology.

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:29.440
And so we believe that
technology is a tool,

00:07:29.440 --> 00:07:33.080
and used correctly, it can
make amazing differences

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:34.130
in the classroom.

00:07:34.130 --> 00:07:37.640
And so part of what I want to
talk about today is how can you

00:07:37.640 --> 00:07:40.202
use technology to make a
difference in the classroom,

00:07:40.202 --> 00:07:41.660
and what are some
of the principles

00:07:41.660 --> 00:07:43.221
that you can adhere to?

00:07:43.221 --> 00:07:44.720
And then talk to
you about how we're

00:07:44.720 --> 00:07:47.810
designing our technology to
adhere to those principles,

00:07:47.810 --> 00:07:48.660
as well.

00:07:48.660 --> 00:07:50.990
And then finally, hear
from a set of educators

00:07:50.990 --> 00:07:53.240
who are actually doing
this and can tell you

00:07:53.240 --> 00:07:56.740
more about what they've seen.

00:07:56.740 --> 00:08:00.670
So at Google, we have really
been involved in education

00:08:00.670 --> 00:08:02.740
from day one.

00:08:02.740 --> 00:08:06.280
Google, if you look at it, was
founded at Stanford University.

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:12.240
Was google.stanford.edu for many
years before it incorporated.

00:08:12.240 --> 00:08:16.690
It was started by
two founders who

00:08:16.690 --> 00:08:19.870
were sons of college
professors, and so it's really

00:08:19.870 --> 00:08:22.720
rooted in education.

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:25.030
From the very first day
when we had Google search,

00:08:25.030 --> 00:08:26.780
schools started
using Google search,

00:08:26.780 --> 00:08:29.200
and that was a key
tool in education

00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:31.600
and has been since the
founding of Google.

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:36.460
And then about 11
years ago, in 2005--

00:08:36.460 --> 00:08:38.710
at the end of 2005,
beginning of 2006--

00:08:38.710 --> 00:08:40.690
we started to
actually think about

00:08:40.690 --> 00:08:42.309
how can we improve the school?

00:08:42.309 --> 00:08:45.190
How can we help schools
bring technology in?

00:08:45.190 --> 00:08:46.450
How can we help teachers?

00:08:46.450 --> 00:08:48.880
And that's when we launched
G Suite for Education,

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:52.600
which at the time was known
as Google Apps for Education.

00:08:52.600 --> 00:08:54.610
It started with Gmail.

00:08:54.610 --> 00:08:56.410
That was the only
app that we had.

00:08:56.410 --> 00:08:59.320
And then it has
expanded to a variety

00:08:59.320 --> 00:09:01.990
of creative and
collaborative applications.

00:09:01.990 --> 00:09:05.020
On top of that, then, we
added Google Classroom,

00:09:05.020 --> 00:09:07.540
which is really kind of the
mission control for all of this

00:09:07.540 --> 00:09:10.270
for a teacher, and
lets them guide

00:09:10.270 --> 00:09:14.110
what's going on in the
classroom in a much better way.

00:09:14.110 --> 00:09:15.880
And then we added Chromebooks.

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:19.750
And one of the key problems that
we heard from a lot of teachers

00:09:19.750 --> 00:09:22.820
was OK, great, I have
all this technology,

00:09:22.820 --> 00:09:28.840
but how do I have my kids get
access to this technology?

00:09:28.840 --> 00:09:32.590
If they can only use it one
hour a week in the computer lab,

00:09:32.590 --> 00:09:34.220
it doesn't really
make a difference.

00:09:34.220 --> 00:09:36.726
They need to be able to
use it on a daily basis

00:09:36.726 --> 00:09:38.350
for me to be able to
change curriculum,

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:40.420
and that's where
Chromebooks really came in--

00:09:40.420 --> 00:09:43.960
giving people an affordable,
manageable, secure,

00:09:43.960 --> 00:09:47.870
and shareable way to bring
computing into the classroom.

00:09:47.870 --> 00:09:50.950
And we've gone beyond that with
things like Google Expeditions,

00:09:50.950 --> 00:09:53.670
making it so that you can have
virtual reality experiences,

00:09:53.670 --> 00:09:56.440
and visit a far-off
land while sitting

00:09:56.440 --> 00:09:58.000
in a school in California.

00:09:58.000 --> 00:10:01.060
Those kinds of experiences
are now possible

00:10:01.060 --> 00:10:02.580
with the technologies
that we have.

00:10:02.580 --> 00:10:05.139
And we're going to continue to
innovate to make things better

00:10:05.139 --> 00:10:05.680
for teachers.

00:10:08.780 --> 00:10:13.465
Now, to figure out how to
best impact education, one

00:10:13.465 --> 00:10:14.840
of the things we
did was actually

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:18.620
took a step back from this
to try to really figure out

00:10:18.620 --> 00:10:23.510
the kinds of principles that
we want to be able to foster.

00:10:23.510 --> 00:10:27.350
And so we actually worked with
the Columbia University Teacher

00:10:27.350 --> 00:10:29.930
College to try to
figure out what

00:10:29.930 --> 00:10:33.040
are some of the characteristics
of really successful schools,

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:35.882
or schools that are using
technology in a great way,

00:10:35.882 --> 00:10:37.340
or schools that
are just succeeding

00:10:37.340 --> 00:10:39.170
in one way or another?

00:10:39.170 --> 00:10:41.270
And can we boil it down
to a set of principles?

00:10:41.270 --> 00:10:44.420
And we came up with
four principles

00:10:44.420 --> 00:10:47.180
that came out of that research.

00:10:47.180 --> 00:10:49.400
So the first thing is
that learning should

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:51.620
be personalized and measured.

00:10:51.620 --> 00:10:55.730
If you tailor the learning to
the needs of the individual,

00:10:55.730 --> 00:10:57.620
they will be able
to learn better, as

00:10:57.620 --> 00:11:00.530
opposed to just teaching
the same thing to everybody

00:11:00.530 --> 00:11:01.670
in the classroom.

00:11:01.670 --> 00:11:04.040
And that's an easy
concept to understand,

00:11:04.040 --> 00:11:06.770
but it's hard to actually do.

00:11:06.770 --> 00:11:09.890
If you're teacher and you have
30 students in your classroom,

00:11:09.890 --> 00:11:12.390
it's very hard to
differentiate the learning

00:11:12.390 --> 00:11:16.850
to every single kid, but this
is where technology can help.

00:11:16.850 --> 00:11:20.150
In addition to that,
learning should be measured.

00:11:20.150 --> 00:11:22.010
And so we think
about measurement

00:11:22.010 --> 00:11:24.770
in a lot of cases as
high-stakes assessments

00:11:24.770 --> 00:11:26.720
that happen at the
end of every year.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:29.610
Measurements should be going
on on a constant basis,

00:11:29.610 --> 00:11:31.820
and really, to give
feedback to the student

00:11:31.820 --> 00:11:35.120
about what they're good at,
what they need help with,

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:36.664
where they can move forward--

00:11:36.664 --> 00:11:37.580
those kinds of things.

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:39.830
And then feed that
back into the education

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:44.700
and tailor the education
even more to their needs.

00:11:44.700 --> 00:11:46.710
So great example
we saw of this is

00:11:46.710 --> 00:11:47.960
Charlotte-Mecklenburg schools.

00:11:47.960 --> 00:11:51.050
And so Charlotte-Mecklenburg
is one of the largest school

00:11:51.050 --> 00:11:52.670
districts in the United States--

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:54.830
140,000 students.

00:11:54.830 --> 00:11:57.080
And they went one to
one with Chromebooks,

00:11:57.080 --> 00:12:00.680
so all of their students
now have Chromebooks.

00:12:00.680 --> 00:12:03.050
And they've been able to
see amazing things that

00:12:03.050 --> 00:12:05.240
have happened in terms of
how they can actually now

00:12:05.240 --> 00:12:08.720
get personal with learning now
that they have Chromebooks.

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:12.950
And so for example, they
now have digital classes

00:12:12.950 --> 00:12:16.174
that students can use if
they fall behind in something

00:12:16.174 --> 00:12:18.590
or if they need to understand
something a little bit more.

00:12:18.590 --> 00:12:21.410
Or they can do live
one-on-one sessions

00:12:21.410 --> 00:12:24.320
when they need it to be able
to interact with the teacher

00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:26.494
when they need help.

00:12:26.494 --> 00:12:27.410
Those kinds of things.

00:12:27.410 --> 00:12:30.890
So a student that's a little
bit behind can catch up.

00:12:30.890 --> 00:12:33.520
They also came with this
idea of the genius hour.

00:12:33.520 --> 00:12:37.040
So students that were ahead
and that were feeling bored--

00:12:37.040 --> 00:12:40.250
they can get a Chromebook
and they can go and research

00:12:40.250 --> 00:12:42.620
anything that they
wanted to research,

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:46.040
and then be able to go and
present that back to the class

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:47.990
as part of this genius hour.

00:12:47.990 --> 00:12:50.030
So amazing things
like that you can only

00:12:50.030 --> 00:12:54.149
do now that you have
technology in the classroom.

00:12:54.149 --> 00:12:55.940
Since they started
implementing technology,

00:12:55.940 --> 00:13:00.140
they saw their graduation
rate increase from 70% to 90%.

00:13:00.140 --> 00:13:02.570
And a lot of that they credit
to the amount of engagement

00:13:02.570 --> 00:13:07.510
that they now see that students
have with school in general.

00:13:07.510 --> 00:13:09.520
Second principle is
that learning should

00:13:09.520 --> 00:13:11.980
be collaborative and diverse.

00:13:11.980 --> 00:13:13.990
And what we mean by
that is that students

00:13:13.990 --> 00:13:15.880
need to learn how
to work together,

00:13:15.880 --> 00:13:18.610
and they also need to learn how
to absorb diverse viewpoints

00:13:18.610 --> 00:13:22.150
and reconcile that together.

00:13:22.150 --> 00:13:25.450
And this is something, again,
where we think that technology

00:13:25.450 --> 00:13:27.790
can really, really help.

00:13:27.790 --> 00:13:31.270
An example of that is McKinnon
Secondary School in Australia.

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:36.280
What they were able to do is now
that they had technology there,

00:13:36.280 --> 00:13:38.200
they were able to bring
in collaborative tools

00:13:38.200 --> 00:13:40.810
with things like Google Docs,
things like Google Classroom.

00:13:40.810 --> 00:13:42.850
Students were starting
to use that to ask

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:47.080
questions of each other, and
help each other with homework.

00:13:47.080 --> 00:13:48.970
They would comment on
each other's documents

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:51.220
and help each other get
better at writing documents.

00:13:51.220 --> 00:13:54.370
And the teachers can actually go
in and comment on the document

00:13:54.370 --> 00:13:56.920
while the student is
writing it to give them

00:13:56.920 --> 00:13:58.390
real-time feedback.

00:13:58.390 --> 00:14:02.260
That kind of feedback is
something where they can now

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:05.890
start to work with each
other and bring it and accept

00:14:05.890 --> 00:14:07.600
a lot of diverse viewpoints.

00:14:07.600 --> 00:14:09.520
And they're a very
diverse school district,

00:14:09.520 --> 00:14:12.190
so they were able to actually
bring in multiple viewpoints

00:14:12.190 --> 00:14:14.880
as a result of that.

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:17.060
The third principle is
that learning should be

00:14:17.060 --> 00:14:19.040
project-based and self-managed.

00:14:19.040 --> 00:14:22.370
And so if you give students
ownership of the learning,

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:25.420
that learning will stick
in a much better way.

00:14:25.420 --> 00:14:28.340
I told you about the
examples that I had,

00:14:28.340 --> 00:14:30.410
about writing
letters to senators

00:14:30.410 --> 00:14:32.330
and presidential candidates.

00:14:32.330 --> 00:14:35.270
I remember that because
I took ownership of that.

00:14:35.270 --> 00:14:37.300
That was something
that was mine.

00:14:37.300 --> 00:14:39.020
And so those are
the kinds of lessons

00:14:39.020 --> 00:14:41.450
that really, really matter.

00:14:41.450 --> 00:14:43.670
An interesting
statistic we saw is

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:48.170
that it is very likely the
vast majority of people that

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:50.060
are graduating from
college right now

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:53.000
will end up down the line
some time in a career that

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.620
doesn't exist today.

00:14:55.620 --> 00:14:57.927
What that means is that
what they learned in college

00:14:57.927 --> 00:14:59.510
will be somewhat
valuable, but they're

00:14:59.510 --> 00:15:02.390
going to have to learn
more and more on the fly

00:15:02.390 --> 00:15:04.860
to be able to do
what they need to do.

00:15:04.860 --> 00:15:08.180
And so that skill of being able
to manage your own learning

00:15:08.180 --> 00:15:10.640
is really, really critical.

00:15:10.640 --> 00:15:14.420
An example we saw of this
is in Maine Township High

00:15:14.420 --> 00:15:16.746
School in Chicago.

00:15:16.746 --> 00:15:18.620
There's a set of kids
there that started what

00:15:18.620 --> 00:15:20.080
they call the Chrome Depot.

00:15:20.080 --> 00:15:22.640
So they were able
to actually create

00:15:22.640 --> 00:15:26.120
their own little business
to be able to help kids

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:29.220
with Chromebooks and help
kids with technology.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:31.360
They did this all on their own.

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:34.160
They started, as you can
see, an actual physical place

00:15:34.160 --> 00:15:35.780
that kids could come to.

00:15:35.780 --> 00:15:38.600
And what happened is that they
had to learn a lot of things

00:15:38.600 --> 00:15:40.580
that nobody could actually
come and tell them.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:42.290
They couldn't go to
a teacher to learn

00:15:42.290 --> 00:15:45.080
how do you repair a Chromebook,
or how do you do x, y,

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:47.000
and z with a such-and-such tool?

00:15:47.000 --> 00:15:49.790
They had to learn that, and they
ended up becoming the experts,

00:15:49.790 --> 00:15:51.890
even more so than
their teachers.

00:15:51.890 --> 00:15:53.810
That kind of learning
is the kind of thing

00:15:53.810 --> 00:15:57.420
that will stick with them
now for a very long time.

00:15:57.420 --> 00:15:59.720
And the fourth principle
is that learning should

00:15:59.720 --> 00:16:02.990
be conceptual and experiential.

00:16:02.990 --> 00:16:06.524
Back to the example of
the list of presidents--

00:16:06.524 --> 00:16:07.940
it's not about the
knowledge, it's

00:16:07.940 --> 00:16:09.860
about what you do
with that knowledge.

00:16:09.860 --> 00:16:12.640
How you take that knowledge
and create concepts about that,

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:15.620
and how do you experience it?

00:16:15.620 --> 00:16:20.110
So an example we saw of that is
[? Olou ?] school in Finland.

00:16:20.110 --> 00:16:23.330
What they wanted to do is
teach their kids about Africa.

00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:25.970
And so I think about how
I learned about Africa.

00:16:25.970 --> 00:16:30.710
I learned about it as a map
with a bunch of countries,

00:16:30.710 --> 00:16:32.270
and learned a little
bit about a few

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:34.010
of the countries here and there.

00:16:34.010 --> 00:16:37.820
What they did is they actually
had their kids plan out a trip

00:16:37.820 --> 00:16:39.297
to Africa.

00:16:39.297 --> 00:16:40.880
So the kids could
figure out a country

00:16:40.880 --> 00:16:42.421
that they wanted to
go to, they would

00:16:42.421 --> 00:16:45.140
have to research that
country using the Chromebooks

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:48.890
that they had in the classroom,
and plan out a trip itinerary.

00:16:48.890 --> 00:16:50.396
And then they
could actually take

00:16:50.396 --> 00:16:51.770
the trip using
Google Expeditions

00:16:51.770 --> 00:16:54.890
and actually experience that
country, be able to go there

00:16:54.890 --> 00:16:58.370
and see what that was like.

00:16:58.370 --> 00:17:01.250
That kind of experience
gives them a much better idea

00:17:01.250 --> 00:17:05.390
of what Africa is
all about, and lets

00:17:05.390 --> 00:17:07.190
them start to not just
have that knowledge,

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:10.700
but also bring it to concepts.

00:17:10.700 --> 00:17:14.910
So then given this, how do we
design technology for schools

00:17:14.910 --> 00:17:17.730
to make it such
that we can adapt

00:17:17.730 --> 00:17:19.470
to these kinds of
environments and help

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:21.770
these kinds of environments?

00:17:21.770 --> 00:17:23.750
So one of the first
things that we've

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:26.030
been realizing, especially
over the last year,

00:17:26.030 --> 00:17:29.040
is that we need to
design for versatility.

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:31.940
How we think, for example,
a device is going to be used

00:17:31.940 --> 00:17:34.400
is maybe not how it's
going to be used,

00:17:34.400 --> 00:17:37.940
especially as students try
to do things by themselves.

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:40.340
As many of you know, when we
first released Chromebooks,

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:41.690
they were released as laptops.

00:17:41.690 --> 00:17:43.231
And most of the
Chromebooks out there

00:17:43.231 --> 00:17:45.580
are essentially in a
laptop form factor.

00:17:45.580 --> 00:17:47.810
But we've been asked by
students and by teachers

00:17:47.810 --> 00:17:50.540
to do all kinds of things
with these devices--

00:17:50.540 --> 00:17:53.310
everything from turning them
into tablets to turning them

00:17:53.310 --> 00:17:56.330
into notebooks to turning them
into books to turning them

00:17:56.330 --> 00:17:59.150
into microscopes, for
example, or turning them

00:17:59.150 --> 00:18:02.960
into virtual windows
into a virtual world--

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:03.990
those kinds of things.

00:18:03.990 --> 00:18:05.870
And so one of the
things that we just did

00:18:05.870 --> 00:18:08.540
is we released a whole
set of Chromebooks

00:18:08.540 --> 00:18:09.920
that are convertible
and that are

00:18:09.920 --> 00:18:11.720
versatile to be
able to turn them

00:18:11.720 --> 00:18:14.750
into a tablet, a
notebook, a textbook,

00:18:14.750 --> 00:18:16.100
and whatever you need it to be.

00:18:16.100 --> 00:18:20.450
So it folds over, you're able
to turn it into a full tablet.

00:18:20.450 --> 00:18:23.090
You have a stylus with it so
you can be able to write on it

00:18:23.090 --> 00:18:25.640
and do all kinds of things.

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:27.470
The hardware is part
of that, but then you

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:29.360
also need to design for
versatility in terms

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:31.100
of the software itself.

00:18:31.100 --> 00:18:34.220
And so one thing that
we recently released

00:18:34.220 --> 00:18:37.310
is the ability for Chromebooks
to run all Android apps.

00:18:37.310 --> 00:18:40.640
And so now, you can run apps
from the Google Play Store,

00:18:40.640 --> 00:18:43.420
and be able to get
access to a whole host--

00:18:43.420 --> 00:18:45.140
millions-- of
applications that can

00:18:45.140 --> 00:18:48.250
do all kinds of interesting
things, especially

00:18:48.250 --> 00:18:49.100
for education.

00:18:49.100 --> 00:18:52.716
And so that has really made
it so that these devices can

00:18:52.716 --> 00:18:55.340
be used for even more scenarios
than we would have ever dreamed

00:18:55.340 --> 00:18:57.380
of.

00:18:57.380 --> 00:18:59.930
The next thing is that
we design to empower.

00:18:59.930 --> 00:19:02.540
How do we empower students
and empower teachers

00:19:02.540 --> 00:19:04.490
to do great things?

00:19:04.490 --> 00:19:06.680
One of the first examples
of this was Google Docs.

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:10.190
We knew that for decades
students and teachers

00:19:10.190 --> 00:19:13.070
collaborate on documents,
and that people

00:19:13.070 --> 00:19:14.570
try to collaborate on papers.

00:19:14.570 --> 00:19:17.420
And when I was in school,
that was I write a paper,

00:19:17.420 --> 00:19:19.790
take that piece of paper,
hand it to my teacher,

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:22.130
they mark it up, and
hand it back to me.

00:19:22.130 --> 00:19:24.240
What we now have is
the ability, of course,

00:19:24.240 --> 00:19:26.360
to be able to
collaborate in real time.

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:30.194
And that makes it so that you
can collaborate with students

00:19:30.194 --> 00:19:31.610
from student to
student or student

00:19:31.610 --> 00:19:33.980
to teacher in a much
more seamless manner.

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:36.860
By designing that as a core
principle of the product,

00:19:36.860 --> 00:19:39.470
we make it so that the product
itself is more powerful

00:19:39.470 --> 00:19:42.180
and fosters that
collaborative nature.

00:19:42.180 --> 00:19:44.780
We're also now starting to
make things even more personal.

00:19:44.780 --> 00:19:48.260
An example of this is Google
Classroom, where we now

00:19:48.260 --> 00:19:50.870
can send you a personal alert,
and send personal alerts

00:19:50.870 --> 00:19:54.920
to parents, as well, about
what the child has been doing

00:19:54.920 --> 00:19:56.840
and how they're doing.

00:19:56.840 --> 00:20:00.540
What are things that are
up on tap for next week?

00:20:00.540 --> 00:20:02.420
What were the activities
for them this week?

00:20:02.420 --> 00:20:03.500
Those kinds of things.

00:20:03.500 --> 00:20:05.660
And be able to give
them a better sense

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:08.420
of their own personal agenda.

00:20:08.420 --> 00:20:10.970
And then we're now even
taking that a step further

00:20:10.970 --> 00:20:13.340
and starting to incorporate
machine learning.

00:20:13.340 --> 00:20:15.800
And we're really just
at the cusp of this,

00:20:15.800 --> 00:20:18.180
and there's a lot more
that I think we can do.

00:20:18.180 --> 00:20:22.270
But an example of this is adding
superpowers to spreadsheets.

00:20:22.270 --> 00:20:24.410
And what I mean by that
is you have a spreadsheet

00:20:24.410 --> 00:20:25.820
with a bunch of numbers--

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:28.800
how do you actually draw
concepts from those?

00:20:28.800 --> 00:20:32.060
So now we have the ability to
actually ask Google a question

00:20:32.060 --> 00:20:37.880
about that spreadsheet, and then
be able to figure out answers.

00:20:37.880 --> 00:20:41.780
So for example, you
can ask, in this case,

00:20:41.780 --> 00:20:44.741
is there a correlation
between age and dexterity?

00:20:44.741 --> 00:20:46.490
And it'll actually
look at the spreadsheet

00:20:46.490 --> 00:20:50.000
and look at the data and be
able to give you back an answer.

00:20:50.000 --> 00:20:52.190
This is useful for
students in a lot of ways

00:20:52.190 --> 00:20:54.570
to draw concepts out of data.

00:20:54.570 --> 00:20:56.300
It's also useful for teachers.

00:20:56.300 --> 00:20:58.610
If a teacher, for
example, uses Google Forms

00:20:58.610 --> 00:21:02.150
to be able to do these
ongoing assessments,

00:21:02.150 --> 00:21:04.730
they can use that to draw
concepts and draw conclusions

00:21:04.730 --> 00:21:06.980
about are there concepts
that they're missing,

00:21:06.980 --> 00:21:09.740
or the whole class is missing,
or a certain set of people

00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:10.316
are missing?

00:21:10.316 --> 00:21:11.690
Those kinds of
things you can now

00:21:11.690 --> 00:21:16.250
start to do because
of machine learning.

00:21:16.250 --> 00:21:18.840
And last but not least,
we design for scalability.

00:21:18.840 --> 00:21:21.990
We talked about access,
which is a key thing.

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:24.120
How do you get access
to this technology

00:21:24.120 --> 00:21:26.640
to every single
student, and make it

00:21:26.640 --> 00:21:30.240
so that they have that
technology available?

00:21:30.240 --> 00:21:32.390
That's not an easy thing to do.

00:21:32.390 --> 00:21:37.080
You know, it's all about
how do you get devices out

00:21:37.080 --> 00:21:38.079
to these students?

00:21:38.079 --> 00:21:39.870
How do you actually
maintain those devices?

00:21:39.870 --> 00:21:42.030
How do you make sure you
don't turn your teaching

00:21:42.030 --> 00:21:45.450
staff into IT support
when you do that?

00:21:45.450 --> 00:21:47.850
And those are principles we
really designed Chromebooks

00:21:47.850 --> 00:21:48.685
around.

00:21:48.685 --> 00:21:50.310
When we designed
Chromebooks, we really

00:21:50.310 --> 00:21:52.050
wanted to take a
step back and make it

00:21:52.050 --> 00:21:56.370
so that we were designing
something that was really,

00:21:56.370 --> 00:21:58.500
kind of, taking computing
to the next level.

00:21:58.500 --> 00:22:02.160
So we made it simple so that
anybody can pick it up and use

00:22:02.160 --> 00:22:03.690
it without any instruction.

00:22:03.690 --> 00:22:07.050
We made it affordable so you can
finally get a computer starting

00:22:07.050 --> 00:22:08.670
at $200.

00:22:08.670 --> 00:22:09.700
We made it secure.

00:22:09.700 --> 00:22:12.120
We turned the security
model on its head

00:22:12.120 --> 00:22:15.750
and added manageability
as a key part of this such

00:22:15.750 --> 00:22:18.210
that you're able to
manage the device

00:22:18.210 --> 00:22:21.192
and make sure that your users
have a secure experience.

00:22:21.192 --> 00:22:22.650
And one last thing,
which we didn't

00:22:22.650 --> 00:22:24.960
realize was critical at the
beginning but was critical,

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:27.060
is sharability.

00:22:27.060 --> 00:22:29.850
I can pick up any
Chromebook and log into it

00:22:29.850 --> 00:22:33.090
and get all of my applications,
all my data, everything,

00:22:33.090 --> 00:22:34.166
in seconds.

00:22:34.166 --> 00:22:36.540
So what that means is you can
have a cart of Chromebooks.

00:22:36.540 --> 00:22:38.880
30 students can walk
into that classroom,

00:22:38.880 --> 00:22:40.560
pick up a Chromebook,
log in, and start

00:22:40.560 --> 00:22:43.050
to do their work within seconds.

00:22:43.050 --> 00:22:45.790
The next class walks in,
they do the same thing.

00:22:45.790 --> 00:22:47.910
And so with 30
Chromebooks, you could then

00:22:47.910 --> 00:22:50.730
serve 180 students
all throughout the day

00:22:50.730 --> 00:22:53.220
as more students walk
into that same classroom.

00:22:53.220 --> 00:22:54.750
Those kinds of
concepts make it so

00:22:54.750 --> 00:22:56.550
that you can start
without having

00:22:56.550 --> 00:23:01.530
to go full one to one
with these devices.

00:23:01.530 --> 00:23:03.600
So I started with
talking about the past,

00:23:03.600 --> 00:23:05.422
and before I bring
up the educators,

00:23:05.422 --> 00:23:07.130
I want to talk a little
about the future.

00:23:07.130 --> 00:23:09.060
So these are my two kids.

00:23:09.060 --> 00:23:10.830
[INAUDIBLE] is my
older daughter.

00:23:10.830 --> 00:23:12.150
She's seven years old.

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:14.640
And [INAUDIBLE]
is my younger son.

00:23:14.640 --> 00:23:16.410
He's four.

00:23:16.410 --> 00:23:19.200
And so as many of us
do, we try to describe

00:23:19.200 --> 00:23:22.140
what we do at work to my kids.

00:23:22.140 --> 00:23:23.400
So I try to tell them.

00:23:23.400 --> 00:23:25.650
They mostly think I just
go to meetings all day,

00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:28.440
which is actually true.

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:32.130
But one day, my daughter
came home from school

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:34.410
and she said, Daddy, do
we have a Chromebook?

00:23:34.410 --> 00:23:36.980
I want to log in with
my Google Apps account.

00:23:36.980 --> 00:23:39.420
And I was just
absolutely shocked

00:23:39.420 --> 00:23:40.800
and thrilled all
at the same time

00:23:40.800 --> 00:23:42.716
because that was my
entire career right there.

00:23:45.465 --> 00:23:47.090
So as I started to
explain this to her,

00:23:47.090 --> 00:23:49.710
she was like, yeah, whatever,
just get me the Chromebook

00:23:49.710 --> 00:23:51.990
so I can start doing
what I want to do.

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:54.720
And so I gave her the
Chromebook, and she logged in

00:23:54.720 --> 00:23:56.970
and she started to do
all kinds of things.

00:23:56.970 --> 00:24:00.150
She uses technology like
i-Ready to start to learn more

00:24:00.150 --> 00:24:01.470
about English and math.

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:03.030
She uses technologies
like Newsela

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:08.290
to have a kid's
view on latest news.

00:24:08.290 --> 00:24:11.970
A variety of other types of
programs, things like Raz-Kids,

00:24:11.970 --> 00:24:13.560
to be able to have
access from home

00:24:13.560 --> 00:24:16.390
to a massive library of content.

00:24:16.390 --> 00:24:19.770
Things like that
that she wants to do.

00:24:19.770 --> 00:24:21.870
She wants to come
home and she wants

00:24:21.870 --> 00:24:24.480
to do these kinds of things,
and that kind of engagement

00:24:24.480 --> 00:24:26.070
and that kind of
love of learning

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:29.490
is the kind of thing that's
going to carry her really far.

00:24:29.490 --> 00:24:32.760
Actually, last weekend,
[INAUDIBLE] and [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:32.760 --> 00:24:36.436
were talking, and [INAUDIBLE]
said something and [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:36.436 --> 00:24:38.310
said something, and she
said, [INAUDIBLE] I'm

00:24:38.310 --> 00:24:41.040
using the literal
meaning of that, not

00:24:41.040 --> 00:24:42.465
the figurative meaning.

00:24:42.465 --> 00:24:45.090
And so [INAUDIBLE], being four,
kind of looked at her strangely

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:46.770
and went off and
played with his trucks.

00:24:46.770 --> 00:24:49.230
But then I asked her,
how did you know that?

00:24:49.230 --> 00:24:50.880
Where did you learn that from?

00:24:50.880 --> 00:24:52.440
Because she's in first grade.

00:24:52.440 --> 00:24:54.690
She said, oh, I learned
it from i-Ready.

00:24:54.690 --> 00:24:56.730
So these are the
kinds of things she's

00:24:56.730 --> 00:24:59.790
able to pick up on her own
now, and move at her own pace.

00:24:59.790 --> 00:25:02.507
And so those kinds of things
are the types of tools

00:25:02.507 --> 00:25:04.590
we're going to be able to
give the next generation

00:25:04.590 --> 00:25:06.870
of students, and give them
a much better experience

00:25:06.870 --> 00:25:08.580
than what we had.

00:25:08.580 --> 00:25:11.070
So with that, I'd
like to actually

00:25:11.070 --> 00:25:13.020
have you hear from
a set of educators

00:25:13.020 --> 00:25:14.550
that are really doing this.

00:25:14.550 --> 00:25:17.010
So I'd like to bring up
Roland Antoine, who's

00:25:17.010 --> 00:25:21.060
the deputy chief of technology
for Dallas Independent School

00:25:21.060 --> 00:25:21.690
district.

00:25:21.690 --> 00:25:23.730
He has a masters in
educational psychology

00:25:23.730 --> 00:25:27.470
and has been in education
in Texas since 2005-- first

00:25:27.470 --> 00:25:29.580
as a teacher, then as
an assistant principal,

00:25:29.580 --> 00:25:32.010
and then he's moved
into technology,

00:25:32.010 --> 00:25:35.670
and now he leads the Dallas
ISD, Information Technology

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:36.460
Department.

00:25:36.460 --> 00:25:39.260
One of the things he did is
he headed the ETE, Elementary

00:25:39.260 --> 00:25:41.010
to Engineers initiative,
to actually start

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:46.080
to put computer science
into the early grades.

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:51.150
And I want to introduce
David Vannasdall, who

00:25:51.150 --> 00:25:55.100
is superintendent of Arcadia
Unified School district.

00:25:55.100 --> 00:25:58.050
He has a doctorate in
urban education leadership,

00:25:58.050 --> 00:26:01.620
and became an educator
in 1992 in Cincinnati

00:26:01.620 --> 00:26:04.560
to start off with, then
moved to Arcadia in 2005,

00:26:04.560 --> 00:26:06.930
and is now the
superintendent there.

00:26:06.930 --> 00:26:11.230
He's also helped launch AUSD's
annual Arcadia Innovation

00:26:11.230 --> 00:26:13.110
Summit, which brings
together schools

00:26:13.110 --> 00:26:15.740
from a variety of different
places in California

00:26:15.740 --> 00:26:18.870
to have free
professional development.

00:26:18.870 --> 00:26:20.400
And so we wanted
to bring them up

00:26:20.400 --> 00:26:23.370
to actually talk about how
they're using technology

00:26:23.370 --> 00:26:24.900
in the schools.

00:26:24.900 --> 00:26:27.690
So thank you very
much for coming.

00:26:27.690 --> 00:26:29.220
Just to start off
with, it would be

00:26:29.220 --> 00:26:31.910
great to get an overview from
you about your districts.

00:26:31.910 --> 00:26:35.514
Just tell us a little bit about
the context of your district.

00:26:35.514 --> 00:26:36.680
DAVID VANNASDALL: All right.

00:26:36.680 --> 00:26:37.320
I'll start off.

00:26:37.320 --> 00:26:39.330
From Arcadia Unified
School District.

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:43.620
And we have about 10,000
students, TK through 12.

00:26:43.620 --> 00:26:46.980
We're located right at the
foothills of the beautiful San

00:26:46.980 --> 00:26:49.410
Gabriel Mountains in
Southern California.

00:26:49.410 --> 00:26:53.160
We are an affluent
neighborhood, which

00:26:53.160 --> 00:26:56.790
comes with some very high
expectations on the school

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:57.960
district.

00:26:57.960 --> 00:27:00.910
Most of our students are hoping
to get into the Ivy League

00:27:00.910 --> 00:27:01.410
schools.

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:06.644
ROLAND ANTOINE: So Dallas
Independent School District.

00:27:06.644 --> 00:27:07.810
Now, how do I describe that?

00:27:07.810 --> 00:27:10.740
We're an urban school district.

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:14.670
We have between
staff and students--

00:27:14.670 --> 00:27:17.670
I like to describe
it as, like, a city.

00:27:17.670 --> 00:27:19.680
So when I talk to
people about it,

00:27:19.680 --> 00:27:23.640
we have 180,000
inhabitants of the city

00:27:23.640 --> 00:27:25.830
that I like to talk
about as Dallas ISD.

00:27:25.830 --> 00:27:28.260
And the reason why I do
that is because if you

00:27:28.260 --> 00:27:31.560
were to put us on a scale of
cities in the state of Texas,

00:27:31.560 --> 00:27:34.326
we'd be in the top 20
based on population.

00:27:34.326 --> 00:27:35.700
So when we think
about things, we

00:27:35.700 --> 00:27:40.770
have to think about scale in
a very interesting manner.

00:27:40.770 --> 00:27:48.330
In our city, we're K-12, have
very diverse populations, OK?

00:27:48.330 --> 00:27:50.420
Mostly low socioeconomic.

00:27:50.420 --> 00:27:52.260
That means free
and reduced lunch.

00:27:52.260 --> 00:27:55.230
So there are also some
significant economic challenges

00:27:55.230 --> 00:27:57.539
that we have to deal with.

00:27:57.539 --> 00:27:59.080
RAJEN SHETH: And so
Roland, you know,

00:27:59.080 --> 00:28:01.450
you're from a very large
urban school district, very

00:28:01.450 --> 00:28:06.150
diverse population, and in that,
how do you reimagine education,

00:28:06.150 --> 00:28:09.220
and what are some of the
challenges that you've hit?

00:28:09.220 --> 00:28:11.650
ROLAND ANTOINE: Well, as
part of a new leadership

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:14.170
team-- and we're, like,
barely a year old--

00:28:14.170 --> 00:28:18.670
our first major imperative
is resource equity.

00:28:18.670 --> 00:28:21.670
And for us, that means
meaningful access to technology

00:28:21.670 --> 00:28:27.130
for everybody everywhere,
because in a city that large,

00:28:27.130 --> 00:28:30.010
you might miss some things
and miss some people.

00:28:30.010 --> 00:28:32.440
The reason why those
things are important

00:28:32.440 --> 00:28:35.110
is for two reasons when
it comes to students.

00:28:35.110 --> 00:28:36.730
First of all, that
every student have

00:28:36.730 --> 00:28:40.060
access to technology as
a tool, because those

00:28:40.060 --> 00:28:43.330
are the things that we
currently use in, you know,

00:28:43.330 --> 00:28:46.760
post-secondary education
and in work environments.

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:49.970
The other one, and I believe
this one to be as important,

00:28:49.970 --> 00:28:53.680
if not more important,
is access to technology

00:28:53.680 --> 00:28:55.720
as subject matter.

00:28:55.720 --> 00:28:59.620
What I mean by that is
areas like computer science,

00:28:59.620 --> 00:29:04.480
like robotics, like
engineering, that involves that.

00:29:04.480 --> 00:29:10.180
Part of what we did in 2015 is
we rolled out computer science

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:12.730
into every middle
school in the district.

00:29:12.730 --> 00:29:15.410
So if you're a
student in Dallas ISD,

00:29:15.410 --> 00:29:19.210
by the time you have
exited eighth grade,

00:29:19.210 --> 00:29:22.270
you will have taken at
least coding or robotics

00:29:22.270 --> 00:29:23.320
at some level.

00:29:23.320 --> 00:29:26.140
Next year, we will be pushing
it down into elementary school

00:29:26.140 --> 00:29:29.494
so that we can actually notch
up the level in middle school.

00:29:29.494 --> 00:29:30.910
The reason why
that's important is

00:29:30.910 --> 00:29:32.980
because in a large
urban school district,

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:37.200
students may not ever encounter
those learning experiences.

00:29:37.200 --> 00:29:38.800
And if we don't do
that, there won't

00:29:38.800 --> 00:29:42.417
be anybody to go to
this conference in 2027.

00:29:42.417 --> 00:29:44.000
DAVID VANNASDALL:
That's a good point.

00:29:44.000 --> 00:29:46.687
RAJEN SHETH: Well,
we appreciate that.

00:29:46.687 --> 00:29:49.270
And David, you know, you're from
a very high-performing school

00:29:49.270 --> 00:29:52.870
district here in California.

00:29:52.870 --> 00:29:55.660
Similarly, what's your
approach to transformation?

00:29:55.660 --> 00:29:57.580
DAVID VANNASDALL:
Rajen, we had to start

00:29:57.580 --> 00:29:59.770
by grieving some
things that have

00:29:59.770 --> 00:30:02.811
been very important to
education for over 100 years.

00:30:02.811 --> 00:30:04.810
The first one was we had
to recognize that we're

00:30:04.810 --> 00:30:06.190
no longer the experts.

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:08.410
Obviously, our students
can now Google anything.

00:30:08.410 --> 00:30:11.170
That's more information
than a teacher has.

00:30:11.170 --> 00:30:12.850
The second one was
that we're no longer

00:30:12.850 --> 00:30:13.990
in control of the learning.

00:30:13.990 --> 00:30:15.880
In fact, we realize that
some of our students

00:30:15.880 --> 00:30:17.560
were learning in spite of us.

00:30:17.560 --> 00:30:19.240
So we were disrupting
their learning

00:30:19.240 --> 00:30:21.310
between the hours
of 8:00 and 3:00.

00:30:21.310 --> 00:30:24.040
And the last one was that
students are no longer

00:30:24.040 --> 00:30:26.230
motivated by grades.

00:30:26.230 --> 00:30:30.670
They sincerely want to have
a purpose and a passion

00:30:30.670 --> 00:30:32.080
for the work they
do, and they're

00:30:32.080 --> 00:30:33.490
looking to have a voice.

00:30:33.490 --> 00:30:36.340
And they're finding that
voice outside of school,

00:30:36.340 --> 00:30:39.190
unfortunately, and
we had a desire

00:30:39.190 --> 00:30:41.810
to help them find that
voice and get a voice,

00:30:41.810 --> 00:30:44.020
and so we call that a device.

00:30:44.020 --> 00:30:47.020
You can call it a device
for whichever you go with,

00:30:47.020 --> 00:30:49.720
but that's actually the source
for students to perform,

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:51.640
to publish, to author.

00:30:51.640 --> 00:30:55.000
And so we went one to one
over a three-year rollout.

00:30:55.000 --> 00:30:57.270
And although we
call it one to one,

00:30:57.270 --> 00:30:59.710
our students-- like many of
you, it's, like, four to one,

00:30:59.710 --> 00:31:03.490
because you have one in your
pocket and a tablet, et cetera.

00:31:03.490 --> 00:31:05.320
So that's why we also,
at the same time,

00:31:05.320 --> 00:31:08.740
rolled out the G Suite for
Education for everyone.

00:31:08.740 --> 00:31:11.380
It's our LMS
because we recognize

00:31:11.380 --> 00:31:13.840
that kids are going
from one device

00:31:13.840 --> 00:31:17.050
to the next all over
campus, space, and place,

00:31:17.050 --> 00:31:19.660
and so it was just
seamless for them

00:31:19.660 --> 00:31:21.760
to interact with their peers.

00:31:21.760 --> 00:31:22.990
RAJEN SHETH: That's great.

00:31:22.990 --> 00:31:25.960
Well, for both of you guys,
what are some examples

00:31:25.960 --> 00:31:28.180
of transformation
that you've seen,

00:31:28.180 --> 00:31:29.989
and where have you seen
this actually work?

00:31:29.989 --> 00:31:31.780
DAVID VANNASDALL: I'll
jump in on something

00:31:31.780 --> 00:31:35.140
that was just very exciting
to witness myself--

00:31:35.140 --> 00:31:39.010
we had a student that, after
his junior year last year,

00:31:39.010 --> 00:31:43.780
decided to go back to China
and spend some time help taking

00:31:43.780 --> 00:31:46.060
care of his aging grandparents.

00:31:46.060 --> 00:31:49.690
And while he was there, he
became increasingly frustrated

00:31:49.690 --> 00:31:53.440
with the fact that three times a
day, he had to spend at least a

00:31:53.440 --> 00:31:57.760
half hour dispensing medication
for his grandparents-- fumbling

00:31:57.760 --> 00:32:00.610
around with the bottles, these
pills you give once a day,

00:32:00.610 --> 00:32:02.290
these twice a day,
three times a day.

00:32:02.290 --> 00:32:03.790
And he couldn't
leave during the day

00:32:03.790 --> 00:32:05.950
because he had to
be there to do that.

00:32:05.950 --> 00:32:08.890
So he came back to
school and decided

00:32:08.890 --> 00:32:12.490
that he was going to commit
his maker space and genius hour

00:32:12.490 --> 00:32:13.180
time--

00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:14.710
and, of course,
having the ability

00:32:14.710 --> 00:32:21.760
to collaborate with G Suite,
he decided to invent and create

00:32:21.760 --> 00:32:25.510
a dispensary for
medication that could

00:32:25.510 --> 00:32:28.810
be done over Wi-Fi on an app.

00:32:28.810 --> 00:32:31.120
And he was able to
create a prototype,

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:33.640
and actually get it to market.

00:32:33.640 --> 00:32:35.390
We'll see where it goes.

00:32:35.390 --> 00:32:38.510
But that's that source of
passion that it started from.

00:32:38.510 --> 00:32:41.230
It was a desire to
have a profound impact

00:32:41.230 --> 00:32:42.130
on the community.

00:32:42.130 --> 00:32:45.754
And obviously not just
his grandparents, but we

00:32:45.754 --> 00:32:47.920
believe it's going to save
a lot of people, as well.

00:32:47.920 --> 00:32:49.086
RAJEN SHETH: That's amazing.

00:32:49.086 --> 00:32:49.890
Very cool.

00:32:49.890 --> 00:32:51.640
Roland, how about you?

00:32:51.640 --> 00:32:53.610
ROLAND ANTOINE: So
for this illustration,

00:32:53.610 --> 00:32:55.300
I'm going to take
you to a second grade

00:32:55.300 --> 00:32:58.960
classroom I recently visited.

00:32:58.960 --> 00:33:02.350
And you don't really know
what's the effect of some

00:33:02.350 --> 00:33:05.620
of the things that you do, kind
of looking for one thing, how

00:33:05.620 --> 00:33:08.830
that actually turns into
something more interesting.

00:33:08.830 --> 00:33:10.632
So I was on the campus.

00:33:10.632 --> 00:33:13.090
I'm peeking into a second grade
classroom, kind of watching

00:33:13.090 --> 00:33:14.360
what kids are doing.

00:33:14.360 --> 00:33:15.400
I like to do that.

00:33:18.050 --> 00:33:21.430
And so the way that we've
set up the Chromebooks

00:33:21.430 --> 00:33:23.260
is where students
are immediately

00:33:23.260 --> 00:33:25.130
taken to a Clever login page.

00:33:25.130 --> 00:33:26.800
So when they log
into the Chromebook,

00:33:26.800 --> 00:33:29.200
we use Google as an
identity provider.

00:33:29.200 --> 00:33:30.580
They go to the
Clever login page,

00:33:30.580 --> 00:33:32.470
and then they just
click an app and go.

00:33:32.470 --> 00:33:35.320
They never have to log in
to anything ever again.

00:33:35.320 --> 00:33:40.220
So the reason I did that was
to save instructional time--

00:33:40.220 --> 00:33:43.950
a very notable objective.

00:33:43.950 --> 00:33:46.150
I'm watching a student,
and they had just

00:33:46.150 --> 00:33:51.380
finished working inside
a math curriculum app.

00:33:51.380 --> 00:33:53.770
And since they had some
spare time in class,

00:33:53.770 --> 00:33:57.490
they immediately flipped
over into code.org,

00:33:57.490 --> 00:34:00.970
which we actually have it
available to all grade levels,

00:34:00.970 --> 00:34:03.150
but formally only
in middle school,

00:34:03.150 --> 00:34:04.990
and starts working
on an exercise

00:34:04.990 --> 00:34:08.949
I guess his teacher had allowed
them to start working on.

00:34:08.949 --> 00:34:12.159
And so this kid has moved
from mathematics to coding,

00:34:12.159 --> 00:34:15.460
and the reason why
that's significant

00:34:15.460 --> 00:34:19.420
is because this student, a
second grader, first of all,

00:34:19.420 --> 00:34:24.139
is learning how to iterate
at a very early age.

00:34:24.139 --> 00:34:28.639
Whenever you start working with
students in those populations,

00:34:28.639 --> 00:34:30.590
failure becomes
this silent killer

00:34:30.590 --> 00:34:34.100
of any type of curiosity,
because everything's a test,

00:34:34.100 --> 00:34:36.290
everything's a grade,
everything is something

00:34:36.290 --> 00:34:38.800
that either I pass or fail.

00:34:38.800 --> 00:34:40.420
The other thing
is he now has been

00:34:40.420 --> 00:34:42.820
exposed to something that
he would not otherwise

00:34:42.820 --> 00:34:44.230
be exposed to.

00:34:44.230 --> 00:34:48.010
So his view of what the
possibilities of his life

00:34:48.010 --> 00:34:49.949
are vastly different
from the student who

00:34:49.949 --> 00:34:52.000
never encounters that.

00:34:52.000 --> 00:34:54.219
He knows of a very
different universe

00:34:54.219 --> 00:34:56.290
where he can create
and do things

00:34:56.290 --> 00:34:58.750
that other second graders in
some other school district

00:34:58.750 --> 00:35:01.030
probably aren't even
aware of, especially

00:35:01.030 --> 00:35:07.240
if you're in an urban area, like
the area where that school was.

00:35:07.240 --> 00:35:08.410
RAJEN SHETH: That's amazing.

00:35:08.410 --> 00:35:10.120
And you bring up
a very good point

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:12.550
about the concept of failure.

00:35:12.550 --> 00:35:15.159
That you almost need to
teach that failure is OK

00:35:15.159 --> 00:35:16.700
and that you need
to learn from that.

00:35:16.700 --> 00:35:18.700
And that brings me
to my next question,

00:35:18.700 --> 00:35:20.980
is that how do you actually
measure the impact of some

00:35:20.980 --> 00:35:21.460
of these things?

00:35:21.460 --> 00:35:22.959
Because traditionally,
a lot of that

00:35:22.959 --> 00:35:24.490
has been done with test scores.

00:35:24.490 --> 00:35:27.320
But how do you measure
things like that?

00:35:27.320 --> 00:35:28.552
How do you measure impact?

00:35:28.552 --> 00:35:30.010
DAVID VANNASDALL:
One of the things

00:35:30.010 --> 00:35:32.860
that I'm not proud of is
in education for way too

00:35:32.860 --> 00:35:35.350
long, we've been
defining learning

00:35:35.350 --> 00:35:37.690
as just the acquisition
of knowledge, you know?

00:35:37.690 --> 00:35:40.120
How much knowledge can
we jam in their head,

00:35:40.120 --> 00:35:41.770
and usually store it in silos--

00:35:41.770 --> 00:35:43.510
math, science, et cetera.

00:35:43.510 --> 00:35:47.230
What we know now is learning
is what takes place--

00:35:47.230 --> 00:35:50.020
well, I should say
authentic learning

00:35:50.020 --> 00:35:54.460
is what happens when knowledge
intersects with application.

00:35:54.460 --> 00:35:58.180
But it happens at a point of
intrinsic motivation, right?

00:35:58.180 --> 00:35:59.250
That's that purpose.

00:35:59.250 --> 00:36:00.820
There's a passion there.

00:36:00.820 --> 00:36:04.039
And I'll give you
a quick example.

00:36:04.039 --> 00:36:06.330
I gave you the example of
the student that did the app,

00:36:06.330 --> 00:36:08.330
and that has a lot of
technology involved,

00:36:08.330 --> 00:36:10.480
but here's one
that's as important.

00:36:10.480 --> 00:36:13.640
I was visiting a student
in the hospital--

00:36:13.640 --> 00:36:15.880
actually, a student's
parents in the hospital--

00:36:15.880 --> 00:36:16.692
from our district.

00:36:16.692 --> 00:36:18.400
And visiting with the
family, and there's

00:36:18.400 --> 00:36:19.690
a knock at the door.

00:36:19.690 --> 00:36:21.550
And two kids come in--

00:36:21.550 --> 00:36:23.590
they didn't know me,
I didn't know them--

00:36:23.590 --> 00:36:25.390
but they were carrying violins.

00:36:25.390 --> 00:36:28.990
And they asked the family if
they could play the patient's

00:36:28.990 --> 00:36:29.730
favorite song.

00:36:29.730 --> 00:36:30.940
Now, the patient was asleep.

00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:33.121
They shared the favorite
song with the students.

00:36:33.121 --> 00:36:34.870
They didn't know that
song, and they said,

00:36:34.870 --> 00:36:36.610
we'll be back on Wednesday.

00:36:36.610 --> 00:36:39.280
So they went back and they
learned and they mastered

00:36:39.280 --> 00:36:40.930
and they worked on this song.

00:36:40.930 --> 00:36:44.020
And they came back on Wednesday,
and the patient was awake,

00:36:44.020 --> 00:36:46.150
and they played the
song beautifully.

00:36:46.150 --> 00:36:48.760
It brought a smile
to this lady's face,

00:36:48.760 --> 00:36:51.370
probably in one of the
lowest points in her life.

00:36:51.370 --> 00:36:53.320
That wasn't for a grade.

00:36:53.320 --> 00:36:56.410
It was not a motivation
to be best in the class.

00:36:56.410 --> 00:36:58.600
It was truly to take the
knowledge and their love

00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:05.020
of music, master a song that
was important to someone,

00:37:05.020 --> 00:37:06.430
and then deliver it.

00:37:06.430 --> 00:37:08.980
And that's where
learning is, and that's

00:37:08.980 --> 00:37:11.200
the project base, that's
the motivation that we're

00:37:11.200 --> 00:37:12.430
trying to tap into.

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:15.579
So you can't measure
that quantitatively,

00:37:15.579 --> 00:37:17.620
but we're trying to figure
out how to measure it,

00:37:17.620 --> 00:37:19.570
because that's
where we're headed.

00:37:19.570 --> 00:37:20.982
RAJEN SHETH: That makes sense.

00:37:20.982 --> 00:37:22.690
ROLAND ANTOINE: We
actually have a couple

00:37:22.690 --> 00:37:25.210
of ways of running at that.

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:27.430
So we do use
BrightBytes Clarity.

00:37:27.430 --> 00:37:30.550
That's our quantitative measure,
because as we kind of move

00:37:30.550 --> 00:37:32.920
along this path, we want
to be able to kind of look

00:37:32.920 --> 00:37:36.560
at how things progress
for individual schools

00:37:36.560 --> 00:37:39.520
so if we do need to consider
different ways of approaching

00:37:39.520 --> 00:37:42.070
things or different
directions to point people in,

00:37:42.070 --> 00:37:44.937
we can do that.

00:37:44.937 --> 00:37:47.020
The reason that we are
doing things the way we are

00:37:47.020 --> 00:37:50.350
is so that we can be very
flexible with the model

00:37:50.350 --> 00:37:54.270
of implementation at
each individual school.

00:37:54.270 --> 00:37:56.730
Now, the qualitative
measures, which are actually

00:37:56.730 --> 00:37:59.320
a little bit more fun.

00:37:59.320 --> 00:38:02.580
So every year, we have
this thing called STEM Day.

00:38:02.580 --> 00:38:04.830
And inside that, we have
this little competition

00:38:04.830 --> 00:38:09.990
called Tech Fest where
students submit projects.

00:38:09.990 --> 00:38:13.230
And I'm a judge.

00:38:13.230 --> 00:38:15.090
The last couple of
years, most of them

00:38:15.090 --> 00:38:18.570
have been interesting
videos, slide presentations,

00:38:18.570 --> 00:38:20.050
things of that nature.

00:38:20.050 --> 00:38:23.310
So as I'm going through
the electronic submissions,

00:38:23.310 --> 00:38:26.580
all of a sudden I start
running into animations

00:38:26.580 --> 00:38:27.585
built using Scratch.

00:38:30.380 --> 00:38:32.990
And understand these are
coming from fifth graders.

00:38:32.990 --> 00:38:37.340
So I'm now beginning to see
this a very different approach

00:38:37.340 --> 00:38:41.450
to using technology
in this exercise.

00:38:41.450 --> 00:38:43.650
And then a couple
of other fun things.

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:46.910
The first year of
our robotics program,

00:38:46.910 --> 00:38:48.890
we had one of our
[INAUDIBLE] teams

00:38:48.890 --> 00:38:52.100
go to nationals, which was
a huge surprise to them.

00:38:52.100 --> 00:38:54.620
And we had two of
our VEX robotics team

00:38:54.620 --> 00:38:56.570
also go to national
competitions.

00:38:56.570 --> 00:38:57.980
And that's just after one year.

00:38:57.980 --> 00:39:01.692
One of the VEX is actually
an elementary school team.

00:39:01.692 --> 00:39:02.400
RAJEN SHETH: Wow.

00:39:02.400 --> 00:39:04.250
That's amazing.

00:39:04.250 --> 00:39:07.100
Well, so what's next for you?

00:39:07.100 --> 00:39:08.600
What are you thinking
about in terms

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:10.080
of you've gotten this far.

00:39:10.080 --> 00:39:11.372
What more do you want to do?

00:39:11.372 --> 00:39:12.830
DAVID VANNASDALL:
One of the things

00:39:12.830 --> 00:39:16.700
that has getting me up early
right now every morning

00:39:16.700 --> 00:39:18.950
is big data.

00:39:18.950 --> 00:39:22.010
I think the first
big jump we had

00:39:22.010 --> 00:39:25.700
was letting go of the
knowledge, and the next one

00:39:25.700 --> 00:39:27.620
was letting go of the
learning-- putting

00:39:27.620 --> 00:39:29.090
that in the hands of kids.

00:39:29.090 --> 00:39:35.660
The next one is really using
big data to drive our decisions.

00:39:35.660 --> 00:39:37.800
And so just an example.

00:39:37.800 --> 00:39:39.980
We have an incubator group
on one of our campuses

00:39:39.980 --> 00:39:44.270
that is currently using
Prediction API to do

00:39:44.270 --> 00:39:47.000
virtual schedules for a
student before they even

00:39:47.000 --> 00:39:48.710
show up at the school.

00:39:48.710 --> 00:39:51.920
And we can optimize their
learning environment

00:39:51.920 --> 00:39:53.630
by knowing that
they're not going

00:39:53.630 --> 00:39:57.110
to be successful with that
class and that teacher.

00:39:57.110 --> 00:39:59.100
Just an example of
what we're doing.

00:39:59.100 --> 00:40:01.370
However, there's a lot
of ethical dilemmas

00:40:01.370 --> 00:40:03.920
that come with that in
the field of education,

00:40:03.920 --> 00:40:06.820
because if I know
that information, what

00:40:06.820 --> 00:40:09.270
is my obligation at that
point to make those changes?

00:40:09.270 --> 00:40:12.770
And does our structures support
those changes at this time?

00:40:12.770 --> 00:40:13.670
Some big hurdles.

00:40:13.670 --> 00:40:16.090
We have to answer them, though,
because we have this data.

00:40:16.090 --> 00:40:17.131
This isn't in the future.

00:40:17.131 --> 00:40:18.347
This is right now.

00:40:18.347 --> 00:40:20.180
The field of education
has more data points,

00:40:20.180 --> 00:40:22.430
I think, than any other
field, and we just

00:40:22.430 --> 00:40:25.252
need to harness them.

00:40:25.252 --> 00:40:26.710
RAJEN SHETH: How
about you, Roland?

00:40:26.710 --> 00:40:28.293
ROLAND ANTOINE: Well,
as I said, we're

00:40:28.293 --> 00:40:30.280
a new leadership team,
and so this imperative

00:40:30.280 --> 00:40:31.780
of resource equity--

00:40:31.780 --> 00:40:34.240
we still have a good ways to go.

00:40:34.240 --> 00:40:36.700
We're working hand-in-hand
with our school leadership

00:40:36.700 --> 00:40:39.670
division to ensure
that by the time

00:40:39.670 --> 00:40:42.640
we get around to
August of this year,

00:40:42.640 --> 00:40:44.770
our campuses are in
a position to where

00:40:44.770 --> 00:40:47.570
that is not an issue anymore.

00:40:47.570 --> 00:40:49.720
So that's the first
stage of that.

00:40:49.720 --> 00:40:54.400
And I just got an email
after we spoke earlier

00:40:54.400 --> 00:40:58.270
that our initiative
for pushing computer

00:40:58.270 --> 00:41:01.330
science into elementary is
going before our board, like,

00:41:01.330 --> 00:41:03.299
tomorrow.

00:41:03.299 --> 00:41:04.090
RAJEN SHETH: Great.

00:41:04.090 --> 00:41:06.715
DAVID VANNASDALL: I'll add, too,
if you don't mind real quick--

00:41:06.715 --> 00:41:07.240
Vision API.

00:41:07.240 --> 00:41:09.739
This is one of the things that
just made so much sense to us

00:41:09.739 --> 00:41:12.935
when it was brought to us,
is that every one of our kids

00:41:12.935 --> 00:41:14.560
is sitting in front
of a camera all day

00:41:14.560 --> 00:41:20.870
long, whether it's their phone
or it's the device, the laptop.

00:41:20.870 --> 00:41:22.540
So, you know,
harnessing the strength

00:41:22.540 --> 00:41:24.580
of Vision API for a teacher.

00:41:24.580 --> 00:41:26.710
They can only look at
one student at a time,

00:41:26.710 --> 00:41:30.910
but knowing the emotions that
is going on across the classroom

00:41:30.910 --> 00:41:32.830
and being able to
be alerted to that

00:41:32.830 --> 00:41:35.380
and intervene before a
behavior even happens--

00:41:35.380 --> 00:41:36.720
I mean, imagine that.

00:41:36.720 --> 00:41:38.650
And we have that
technology right now.

00:41:38.650 --> 00:41:41.110
So just being able to go
to scale with it and use

00:41:41.110 --> 00:41:42.700
it is very, very exciting.

00:41:42.700 --> 00:41:43.990
RAJEN SHETH: Yeah, definitely.

00:41:43.990 --> 00:41:44.890
DAVID VANNASDALL: And
I'm hoping someone out

00:41:44.890 --> 00:41:46.720
there is going to
take our big data

00:41:46.720 --> 00:41:51.229
and use TensorFlow to just
do some dynamic things.

00:41:51.229 --> 00:41:53.020
RAJEN SHETH: Well, so
last question for you

00:41:53.020 --> 00:41:56.770
is that, obviously, you have
the technology that's there,

00:41:56.770 --> 00:41:58.600
but you have a lot
of constituents.

00:41:58.600 --> 00:42:01.510
You have the students,
the teachers, the parents,

00:42:01.510 --> 00:42:02.770
the community.

00:42:02.770 --> 00:42:06.999
How do you bring everybody
along on this journey?

00:42:06.999 --> 00:42:08.290
ROLAND ANTOINE: Very carefully.

00:42:11.480 --> 00:42:14.680
There's a lot of communication
that has to occur,

00:42:14.680 --> 00:42:17.610
because you're not only talking
about students and teachers

00:42:17.610 --> 00:42:19.360
and administrators,
but, of course, you're

00:42:19.360 --> 00:42:22.360
talking about board members
in a large district.

00:42:22.360 --> 00:42:26.410
Everything can become
political, so you

00:42:26.410 --> 00:42:30.700
have to be able to communicate
very clearly about things.

00:42:30.700 --> 00:42:34.330
Also being able to demonstrate
some victories-- again,

00:42:34.330 --> 00:42:39.670
like robotics
competitions; again, places

00:42:39.670 --> 00:42:42.370
where people can encounter
students actually doing

00:42:42.370 --> 00:42:45.625
the things that you're trying
to explain to them verbally.

00:42:48.150 --> 00:42:51.859
That's how you do that.

00:42:51.859 --> 00:42:52.900
RAJEN SHETH: Makes sense.

00:42:52.900 --> 00:42:55.066
DAVID VANNASDALL: Yeah, the
more school increasingly

00:42:55.066 --> 00:42:58.380
looks different than when
parents were in school,

00:42:58.380 --> 00:43:01.740
we have to do more and more to
educate parents and bring them

00:43:01.740 --> 00:43:02.880
along.

00:43:02.880 --> 00:43:03.930
It scares them.

00:43:03.930 --> 00:43:05.910
All of these
transitions-- the kids

00:43:05.910 --> 00:43:09.930
are more fluent on the
technology than they are.

00:43:09.930 --> 00:43:11.490
I believe schools
are having to work

00:43:11.490 --> 00:43:15.030
as hard to educate parents and
bring them along as students.

00:43:15.030 --> 00:43:17.717
And for us, the
data, the big data--

00:43:17.717 --> 00:43:19.050
and this is just in here, right?

00:43:19.050 --> 00:43:22.455
So no one-- you know,
it scares our parents.

00:43:22.455 --> 00:43:24.210
It completely
scares them when you

00:43:24.210 --> 00:43:27.990
start talking about sharing
their data and using it.

00:43:27.990 --> 00:43:31.410
But I think if we bring them in
and share with them how we're

00:43:31.410 --> 00:43:33.750
using it and the
amount of predictions

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:36.180
that we can make that's going
to increase and optimize

00:43:36.180 --> 00:43:37.589
their student's
learning, I think

00:43:37.589 --> 00:43:38.880
they'll be right there with us.

00:43:38.880 --> 00:43:41.160
But we have to do a better
job of bringing them along.

00:43:41.160 --> 00:43:42.410
RAJEN SHETH: That makes sense.

00:43:42.410 --> 00:43:46.240
[MUSIC PLAYING]

